@verbs update dest inv pathof creator coder shadows debug dump find call goback
@tier coder
@title looking at live objects and changing them

Where these verbs take an object, they take it the way every
creator verb does: a name, 'here', 'me', 'all in here', another
player. Where they take a file, the name is read from the directory
you are standing in unless it starts with '/' or '~'.

  update [<file|object>]

Destructs the loaded copy and loads it again from disk. With no
argument, or with 'here', it updates the place you are in. It does
not reach clones already alive, nor objects that inherit the file
you updated.

  dest <object>
  dest query <object>

Destructs it. Interactive objects are refused. The 'query' form
asks before each one.

  inv <object>          what that object is carrying, with file names.
  pathof <object>       the file name of the object itself.
  creator <object>      who owns the file, and its uid and euid.
  shadows <object>      the shadows acting on it, if any.
  debug <object>        when it was compiled, how big its program is,
                        how many variables and call-outs it has, and
                        which functions it calls but does not define.

'coder' does the same as 'creator', 'dump' the same as 'debug'.

  find <function>() <object>

Says whether that object answers to that function, and which file
defines it. Also reports a shadow that defines it.

  call <function>(<args>) <object>

Calls the function and prints what came back. Arguments are written
as LPC: numbers as numbers, strings in quotes, arrays in braces,
and text between vertical bars is resolved to objects. Every call
is logged.

  goback

Returns you to the place you were in before the last teleport.
